          %title The small cardinality selection axiom
          %h1#the_small_cardinality_selection_axiom The small cardinality selection axiom
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#idea"} Idea
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#relationship_to_other_forms_of_choice"} Relationship to other forms of choice
              %li
                %a{:href => "#consequences"} Consequences
              %li
                %a{:href => "#models"} Models
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The small cardinality selection axiom (SCSA) is a weak form of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+choice"} axiom of choice
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+set"} small
            way”. It was introduced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"} Michael Makkai
            for the study of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stack"} stack
            completions.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p The “global version” of SCSA states:
          %ul
            %li
              The (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+category"}> large
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets"} sets
              is
              %a{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories#StrongEquivalence"} strongly equivalent
              to a full subcategory of itself, each of whose isomorphism classes contains only a set of objects.
          %p
            Therefore, we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/class"} class
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
            assigning to each set
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\Vert A\Vert}
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bijection"} bijection
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ≅
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \cong {\Vert A \Vert}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {\Vert{-}\Vert}
            takes only set-many values on each isomorphism class in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
          %p
            There is also a “local version” of SCSA which can be stated as follows. Given a function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo →
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f:A\to I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi g
                  %mo :
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi J
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g:B\to J
            equipped with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo :
                  %mi J
                  %mo →
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi:J\to I
            is a
            %em local pullback
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            if there is a pair of pullback squares
