          %h2#relationship_to_other_forms_of_choice Relationship to other forms of choice
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                In the presence of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axiom+of+global+choice"} axiom of global choice
                in
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/material+set+theory"} material set theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
                has a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+ordinals"} von Neumann cardinals
            %li
              %p
                Global SCSA also follows from the “global” version of the axiom of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/small+violations+of+choice"} small violations of choice
                %a{:href => "#Makkai"} Makkai’s paper
                (attributed to the referee). Makkai claims there is a local form of SCSA that follows from the local SVC. I have not attempted to show that this is true for the above local SCSA, although it is a guess at the sort of axiom Makkai might have had in mind.
          %h2#consequences Consequences
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                SCSA implies that the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anabicategory"} anabicategory
                of categories and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anafunctors"} anafunctors
                is
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+small+category"} locally essentially small
                and
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,A
                there is a small set of anafunctors from
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                such that any such anafunctor is isomorphic to one in this set. The idea is that we can replace any anafunctor by a saturated one, in which case the set of “specifications that
                = succeed "\u201D" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi F
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %mi a
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x)=a
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi a
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in X, a\in A
                is, if nonempty, a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torsor"} torsor
                over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Iso
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Iso(a,a)
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi Iso
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi a
                        %mo ,
                        %mi a
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %msub
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mrow
                            %mi a
                            %mo ∈
                            %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Iso(a,a))_{a\in A}
              %p
                This implies that there exists a full subcategory of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Cat
                        %mi ana
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana}(X,A)
                whose inclusion is a weak equivalence, and that an exponential
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi A
                        %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^X
                exists. If local SCSA holds in “functional” form, meaning there is a class-function specifying weakly terminal local reindexings, then this implies that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Cat
                        %mi ana
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat_{ana}
                is weakly equivalent (i.e. ana-equivalent) to a locally small bicategory and that it admits a functor
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ↦
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,A) \mapsto A^X
          %h2#models Models
          %p
            %a{:href => "#Makkai"} Makkai
            states only the global SCSA, but claims that there is a local version of it that holds in all Grothendieck toposes. The local form of SCSA stated above is a guess at the one Makkai might have had in mind. I have not attempted to verify that it is true in all Grothendieck toposes.
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Makkai
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Makkai"}> Michael Makkai
